to an expensive style of domestic  expenditure sometime in 1721 the teenage  flamboyant budding doctor was summoned  by a rich quaker named ebenezer to treat  his colleague but the young wife of the  patient tabitha happened to cast a  favory lear at john in medical terms she  rather had the hot spin a silent  exchange of glances opens the congress  to a more substantial intercourse  unfortunately ebenezer walked in on john  and tabitha during said substantial  intercourse quick of limbs and brains  john scrambled out of bed holding a pen  knife in his hands not for self-defense  but to support the lamest of excuses he  was merely treating tabitha to remove  the corns from her feet as ebenezer  processed this pile of steamy manure  john sprinted out of the bedroom he  bolted so fast and so far that he left  norwich and continued running until  london where he resumed his medical  studies taylor studied at st thomas  hospital under surgeons william  chesselden and john de saglier who  advised him to specialize in eye surgery  according to chesterton taylor was a  diligent student who passed his  examinations with all becoming exactness  the young graduate was appointed chief  surgeon in a norwich hospital where he  first tried his hand at lithotomies or  the removal of gallstones but he soon  directed his interest towards  ophthalmology establishing a good  reputation as a counter of cataracts  allow me to clarify here couching was an  old method to treat cataracts which are  cloudy areas in the lens of the eye  which impair the patient's vision  couching consisted in perforating the  patient size outer layer or cornea with  a sharp hooked needle by using this  implement the surgeon would then  displace the lens of the eye by pushing  it downwards and backwards this  procedure basically shifted the cataract  away from the pupil partially restoring  vision medical literature reassures this  that this operation was relatively  painless but between you and me i'd  never allow this dude to come near my  eyes with a sharp hook but the good folk  of nourish were apparently steelier than  me and dozens of patients had their  sight restored by a young doctor  taylor's experience allowed him to  author his first medical treatise an  account of the mechanism of the eye  published in 1727 the preface of this  book first mentions what will become  taylor's personal motto cui sum v tam  dat latin for who gives sight gives life  surely an ambitious statement but that's  in line with his character not happy  with the limitations of the english  language taylor even coined a new term  to refer to himself combining the greek  words for i and physician optomata  in that same year 1727 taylor left  norwich setting out on a long tour of  britain ireland and continental europe  it's not clear why he decided to leave  his hometown and embark on such a tour  according to author dr george coates it  was common at the time for oculus to  move constantly to avoid the ire of  their patients the couching procedure  did restore sight but improvement was  often only temporary thus surgeons left  town before the negative end results  became obvious we know from other  sources that taylor had the habit of  overselling his skills over promising  miraculous results in lengthy orations  before obscuring his patient's corneas  it's logical then that he preferred to  practice his craft as an itinerant  oculus cashing in and leaving town while  still a hero and so it was that  opthalmata taylor toured extensively  through the british isles until 1733  before crossing the channel into france  visiting also holland and switzerland  his tactic of getting the heck out of  there at the right time seemed to work  as his movements were preceded by  favorable newspaper articles or even  endorsements by celebrities of the time  such as swiss physician and poet albert  von haler taylor's reputation within the  medical community continued to grow and  in 1734 in 1735 he received four  honorary medical doctorates from  universities in france switzerland and  cologne in modern-day germany in 1736  our doctor returned to london his fame  such that he was appointed royal oculus  by king george ii surely this level of  fame was met by adequate compensation  but john seemed to have a talent for  squandering all of his earnings getting  into debt and not paying suppliers for  example in 1736 he was sued by one mrs  nugent in london as he lodged his  convalescing patients in nugent's house  but failed to settle the bill later in  1736 he crossed the channel again either  pursuing more fame and fortune or  escaping creditors after a brief stay in  paris taylor visited berlin a local  newspaper at the time reported the  taylor was so deep in debt that he had  to flee his residence at night and  without a lantern following more travel  across the german land and france they  across the pyrenees and arrived in  madrid in october 1737. for the  following five years he continued to  perform surgery in the iberian peninsula  amassing more praise and even meeting  philip the fifth of spain and john the  fifth portugal by 1742 he styled himself  john taylor esquire a cultist to the  king a knight of the order of portugal  doctor of the physic he later wrote to  have celebrated his greatest trials  between madrid and lisbon and yet the  great oculus still couldn't keep still  between 1742 and 1749 he launched  another tour of the british isles  garnering plenty of favorable press  along the way journalist h cross grove  wrote in 1742 i was an eyewitness to his  restoring to sight two persons at my  house on thursday last further 12  patients signed a document testing that  dr john taylor oculus to the king  recovered our perfect sight after having  entirely lost it many years  sure positive reviews from swiss poets  journalists and patients didn't do any  harm but dr taylor had a knack for what  we today call marketing as a good  marketer he understood that he needed  support from the true influences of the  time the aristocracy for his next tour  of europe his strategy would be to  impress local nobility with live  demonstrations of his scientific  knowledge and surgical prowess served  with a copious topping of extravagant  elegance and flair in july of 1749  taylor left london reaching the hague  early in august he traveled with a  personalized carriage decorated with  pictures of eyes and emblazoned with his  motto who gives sight gives life he was  surrounded by a platoon of livery  lackeys who spread the word of taylor's  medical miracles already on august the  18th a local paper reported how the  oculus had impressed the prince and  princess of orange by restoring the  sight of their groom of the chambers the  friendly journalist even reported that  he fitted a blind man with an artificial  eyeball of his own invention with the  aid of which the man is once again able  to see to a certain extent and even to  read six days later the ruling prince  and princess conferred on him the title  of their oculus in ordinary with the  backing of such influences he certainly  was trending taylor moved to amsterdam  in september where an overenthusiastic  journal claimed that he had restored  sight to more than 440 persons without  any accident having befallen a single  one taylor had influencers and reviewers  on his side and he was set to face a  radiant future but that's when the  experts chimed in those bloody spore  sports the inspector of the medical  faculty in amsterdam issued a statement  claiming that the operations undertaken  by taylor had been fruitless resulting  in nothing but sad results by the end of  the month the foster chase etung a  newspaper previously favorable to taylor  dealt the final blow to his reputation  the article reported that all the  favorable testimonials flaunted by the  occultist had actually been bought for  cash having heard there's an angry mob  assembled under his lodgings it's not  clear if they carried torches and  pitchforks but the article claims they  were ready to attack the eye doctor  luckily for him taylor was able to  sneakily evade the siege displaying an  enviable amount of cheek the ops almata  promptly sued the dutch medical faculty  for libel taylor complemented the  lawsuit by spinning some favorable pr on  october 14 1749 a newspaper in cologne  published a heartfelt defense of our  good eye doctor victim of vicious  attacks by ignorant and envious rivals  the trick must have worked as two months  later taylor and his entourage entered  cologne for another tour of tranquil  talks demonstrations and miraculous  surgery the german states welcomed the  english oculus with open arms it seems  by this time he had started introducing  himself as chevalier this is an  aristocratic title meaning knight it is  disputed whether he actually deserved  such title although taylor did claim to  have been knighted by the king of  portugal he also had a diamond-studded  cross bequeathed by the monarch to prove  it in january 1750 taylor operated on  one hair hurt bearing the fancy title of  counsellor of the high court of justice  to his syrian highness the land grave of  hess darmstadt according to a newspaper  article dr taylor restored the  councillor's site without the least  accident curiously the paper was fosiche  setung the same publication which had  utterly cancelled and destroyed our  friend just a few weeks earlier  the german tour continued with a visit  to leipzig on march 27th this was the  place of residence of one of the  greatest composers of all time joanne  sebastian bark now well into his 60s  bark's vision had been steadily  worsening the exact diagnosis is not  clear according to dr richard zieger's  department of ophthalmology university  of amsterdam bark likely suffered from  myopia and cataracts the musician was  worried by the idea of completely losing  his sight advised by his friends he  agreed to seek treatment from the  celebrity itinerant ophthalmic taylor  first operated on the genius composer  sometime between the 28th and 31st of  march 1750 again the exact nature of the  surgery is not known but it most likely  was taylor's standing couching procedure  for cataracts if that was the case buck  was operated on while sitting on a chair  anaesthetics were not commonly used at  the time and oculus sometimes relied on  copious amounts of alcohol or opiates  taylor had another preferred method he  would use a spatula to press the upper  eyelid against the eyeball thus numbing  the nerve endings in the area an  assistant most likely held the musician  firmly in place to avoid sudden jerking  movements after the couching procedure  taylor normally wrapped his patient's  eyes with a tight bandage and prescribed  a course of laxatives and bloodletting  our good friends at vasiche zedong  reported that bark was able to see much  better after the first operation but it  must have been a temporary effect or a  plain lie spun by taylor in fact between  the fifth and the seventh of april  taylor was called to operate on bark a  second time the cobos biographies  indicate that after the second  intervention he was rendered completely  blind fell ill and endured pain in his  eyes these effects are consistent with  many post-operative complications of the  couching procedure such as an eye  hemorrhage or retinal detachment it  seems like taylor had botched both  operations especially the second one not  only was bach unable to perform alright  music but his general health rapidly  declined on july 28 1750 the master of  baroque music succumbed to a stroke  followed by a lethal bout of what was  described as burning fever it was  reported that he suddenly recovered his  vision days before his death but this  could have been a case of charles bonnet  syndrome in which patients experience  complex visual hallucinations so could  it be that chevalier taylor's surgical  procedures may have caused the premature  death of johann sebastian bark well  according to dr ziegers it is unlikely  that bark's burning fever resulted from  surgical complications he does however  point out that the operations  bloodletting and or purgatives would  have weakened him and predisposed him to  new infections  taylor left leipzig on the 8th of april  made a stop in potsdam then headed  towards berlin aiming to impress the  prussian royal court  he didn't while in potsdam he had  operated on two partially blind women  completing the job as in he totally  blinded them news troubled fast and his  royal majesty frederick ii aka frederick  the great took an interest in the case  according to a local paper the king most  graciously issued the order for the said  taylor to leave berlin the sooner the  better so that no other persons who  suffer misfortune with their eyes it was  also reported that frederick most  graciously threatened to string taylor  by the neck should he ever get near his  subject's eyes taylor resurfaced in  dresden in june as usual traveling  faster than bad news in 1751 he was  called to rostock to restore the sight  of the duke of mekelberg schwerin who  suffered from vitis that's inflammation  of the middle layer of the eye not only  did he fail but the duke's personal  physician dr eschenbach found that the  portuguese cross sported by taylor was a  mere piece of jewellery not a badge of  knighthood undeterred the phony  chevalier continued to tour european  capitals from warsaw to saint petersburg  from copenhagen to rome landing in the  eternal city in 1755 taylor had the  chance to impress none other than pope  benedict xiv who actually nobilated him  the now not-so-phony chevalier continued  to meander through europe before  returning to britain in august of 1758  he was reported as staying in the town  of tunbridge well southeast of london  and here is where he may have operated  on another giant of classical music  george frederick handel but let's take  this one with a pinch of salt the only  source for this procedure is a passing  reference in taylor's autobiography and  a poem published in the london chronicle  on august 24th it reads on the recovery  of the site of the celebrated mr handle  by the chevalier taylor in this poem the  muse uterp invokes apollo and the god of  medicine escapists for them to restore  handle sight but apollo replies that  their help is not needed the chevalier  tailor will fix handel's eyes by all  accounts the composer's site continued  to worsen alongside his general health  and he died on april 13 1759. it's not  clear if taylor had a hand in that but  we are inclined to speculate that the  operation never took place and the poem  was an early example of product  placement in a piece of fiction this  marketing tactic may not have worked  after all as it appears that taylor was  in dire financial straits while in  london he was forced to pawn that very  portuguese cross he had sported so  proudly and a notice published in the  same year stated that a certain flaring  itinerant oculist has run from his bail  this is to acquaint him that if he does  not make immediate satisfaction to his  son his name will be published and a  reward for taking him he also left his  wife in distress to whom he has been  married between 30 and 40 years that's  right despite a grueling schedule of  travels and eye operations taylor had  found the time to marry and sire a son  john jr who wasn't exactly happy about  his dad's spending sprees in 1761 john  junior wrote that he had to financially  support his mother as for john cena he  was reported in a list of fugitives for  debt and beyond the seas  [Music]  little is known of taylor's later years  apparently in an ironic twist of fate  his own vision had started to decline  leading to more botched operations in  1769 he established a practice in prague  but the decline was irreversible he was  given an advice to leave by authorities  with a ban on performing eye surgery in  the habsburg territories after years of  disappointments it was time for the  curtains to close or the eyelids to shut  if you like the exact circumstances of  chevalier john taylor's death are  unknown according to musician charles  bernie the two met in rome for dinner in  november 1770. the oculus died there a  few days later on the 16th according to  other witnesses he died in paris several  london papers reported that taylor had  died in july 1772 to quote having given  sight to many thousands the celebrated  chevalier taylor lately died blind at a  very advanced age in a convent a prague  it's tempting to dismiss taylor's death  as the fitting end to a career of  quakery in a stroke of poetic justice  the energetic narcissistic extravagant  restorer of psy died old alone  presumably broke and completely blind  but let's not judge him too soon as a  complete quack nor merely as the man who  blinded and possibly killed bach unlike  other swindling healers of the era  taylor had actually studied medicine and  reportedly quite well while his failures  rather than triumphs have stood the test  of time his success rate was no  different than that of other oculists in  the 1700s medical science at the time  was simply not advanced enough to cure  every disease of the eye and there is at  least one recorded case of a patient  william smythe who feigned blindness  after being treated by taylor to avoid  paying his fees more importantly the  chevalier left behind a corpus of  medical literature which is still  considered valid by ophthalmologists in  the 21st century for example they  developed the first correct diagram of  semi-decussation this is the divergence  of optic nerve fibers he was also the  first oculus to correctly describe how  cataracts occur in the crystalline humor  the fluid-like substance in the lens of  the eye and he may have been the first  to successfully treat strabismus finally  taylor perfected the new procedure of  optical iridotomy to treat glaucoma and  author the first pictorial atlas of eye  diseases perhaps his career can be best  summarized by the judgment bestowed on  him by the already quoted george coates  in professional matters his knowledge  was good he was a shrewd observer and  not without original ideas but his  actual practice was deeply tainted with  the dishonest arts of the quack many  elements go to the formation of the  complete charlatan bombast effrontery  dishonesty ignorance all these qualities  taylor showed imperfection except  ignorance and this is his chief  condemnation

